@page "/science"
@inject AppData Data

<PageTitle>The science · PaleCheck</PageTitle>

@if (_m is null || _model is null)
{
    <p class="muted">Loading results…</p>
    return;
}

<p class="kicker">Research notes</p>
<h1>We tried to build an AI that spots anaemia in an eyelid photo. Here is what we actually found.</h1>
<p class="lede">
    Nurses screen for anaemia by pulling down the lower eyelid and judging how pale it is. Phone apps promise to do the same
    with AI. Before trusting any number, we audited the largest public dataset, validated honestly, and measured what room
    lighting does to the colour. Every result below is computed by <code>model/train.py</code> and reproduced on this page.
</p>

<nav class="toc">
    <a href="science#audit">1 · Data audit</a>
    <a href="science#accuracy">2 · Honest accuracy</a>
    <a href="science#lighting">3 · Lighting</a>
    <a href="science#screening">4 · Should a photo decide?</a>
    <a href="science#meaning">5 · What it means</a>
    <a href="science#methods">6 · Methods</a>
    <a href="science#protocol">7 · Data protocol</a>
</nav>

<section class="panel" id="audit">
    <h2>1 · @((_m.Audit.ShareUnreliable * 100).ToString("0"))% of the benchmark is reused photos</h2>
    <p>
        CP-AnemiC (@_m.Audit.Images eyelid photos of children in Ghana, each with a lab haemoglobin value) is the largest public
        dataset of its kind. Hashing every image's pixels showed that <strong>@_m.Audit.ImagesInDuplicateGroups images</strong>
        fall into <strong>@_m.Audit.DuplicateGroups groups of identical photos</strong>. In every one of those groups, the copies
        are listed as <em>different children</em> — different hospital, age, sex or haemoglobin.
    </p>
    <div class="dup">
        <figure>
            <img src="science/duplicate-example.png" alt="One eyelid photo that appears under @_m.Audit.LargestGroup patient records" />
            <figcaption>One photo, @_m.Audit.LargestGroup patient records.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Hb g/dL</th><th>Age (mo)</th><th>Sex</th><th>Hospital</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                @foreach (var r in _m.Audit.Example)
                {
                    <tr class="@(r.Hb < 11 ? "anaemic-row" : "")">
                        <td>@r.Id</td><td>@r.Hb.ToString("0.0#")</td><td>@r.AgeMonths</td><td>@r.Gender</td><td>@r.Hospital</td>
                    </tr>
                }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The same pixels carry haemoglobin values up to <strong>@_m.Audit.MaxHbSpread.ToString("0.0") g/dL apart</strong>.
        At most one record per group can be right, so none of them can be trusted.
    </p>
    <h3>Then our own tools found two more layers</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Re-encoded copies.</strong> We built <a href="dupescope">DupeScope</a> to make the check reusable. Its
            near-duplicate test found <strong>@_m.Audit.NearAdded more images</strong> in @_m.Audit.NearClusters clusters: resized or
            re-saved copies of another record's photo. Some sat on both sides of <em>our own</em> development/lockbox split.</li>
        <li><strong>Re-cropped copies.</strong> In the <a href="benchmark">Benchmark Lab</a>, a memoriser that only copies the label of
            the most similar image still scored far above our model on the "clean" data. Its nearest neighbours were often
            consecutive image numbers. They turned out to be <strong>the same photograph cut out with a different outline</strong>:
            sliding one over the other, their overlapping pixels match almost exactly. This added <strong>@_m.Audit.RecropAdded more
            images</strong> in @_m.Audit.RecropClusters clusters (@_m.Audit.MirroredPairs pairs are mirror-image copies), every cluster
            again filed as different children.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="stats compact">
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.Audit.Images</b><span>patient records in the dataset</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.Audit.DistinctPhotographs</b><span>distinct photographs behind them</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.Audit.ReliableKept</b><span>images whose photograph belongs to one record only</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
        We checked the re-crop detector against a control: the same candidate pairs with one image mirrored, which keeps colour
        and texture statistics but breaks any real pixel match. At our threshold the control only ever matched true mirror-image
        copies (difference 0); the next-closest control pair sat above the threshold. Every time the rule changed we re-ran
        the whole protocol, and all three runs are reported:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Images kept</th><th>Nested-CV AUC</th><th>Lockbox AUC (95% CI)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            @foreach (var v in _m.ProtocolHistory)
            {
                <tr class="@(v == _m.ProtocolHistory[^1] ? "chosen" : "")">
                    <td>v@(v.Version): @v.Rule</td>
                    <td>@v.ReliableImages</td>
                    <td>@v.NestedCvAuc.ToString("0.00")</td>
                    <td>@v.LockboxAuc.ToString("0.00") (@v.LockboxAucCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@v.LockboxAucCi95[1].ToString("0.00"), n = @v.LockboxN)</td>
                </tr>
            }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
        Nested cross-validation, the steadiest estimate, stays at about 0.65 in every version. The lockbox moves because each
        version draws a new, smaller random lockbox, and with @_m.ProtocolHistory[^1].LockboxN images its confidence interval is
        wide. In total <strong>@_m.Audit.Unreliable of @_m.Audit.Images images (@((_m.Audit.ShareUnreliable * 100).ToString("0.0"))%)</strong>
        are excluded. Rules: near-identical — @_m.Audit.NearRule; re-crop — @_m.Audit.RecropRule.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>Why it matters:</strong> under ordinary random 5-fold cross-validation,
            <strong>@((_m.Audit.LeakedShare * 100).ToString("0"))%</strong> of test photos have an identical twin in the training set.
            A "model" that only memorises photos — and knows nothing about colour — scores
            <strong>@((_m.Audit.MemoriserAccuracy * 100).ToString("0.0"))% accuracy</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">For context: @_m.Audit.PublishedBenchmark We do not claim those results are wrong — only that this
            protocol cannot separate learning from memorising.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="benchmark">🧪 Watch a model cheat (Benchmark Lab)</a>
        <a class="btn" href="dupescope">🔎 Check your own dataset with DupeScope</a>
        <a class="btn" href="data/cp-anemic-reliable.csv" download>⬇ @_m.Audit.ReliableKept reliable images (CSV)</a>
        <a class="btn" href="data/cp-anemic-unreliable-groups.csv" download>⬇ @_m.Audit.Unreliable excluded images, grouped (CSV)</a>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="accuracy">
    <h2>2 · Honest accuracy: AUC ≈ @_m.NestedCvAll.AucMean.ToString("0.00"), and very uncertain</h2>
    <p>
        Only @(_m.Dataset.Anaemic + _m.Dataset.Healthy) independent images remain for training and testing (plus 8 demo images), so
        every estimate is uncertain. Protocol fixed in advance: 8 demo images set aside, then a <strong>lockbox</strong> of
        @_m.Lockbox.N images evaluated exactly once; all choices (feature set, regularisation, thresholds) were made on the
        @_m.Dataset.Development development images only. The steadiest number is nested cross-validation over all of them.
    </p>
    <div class="stats compact">
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.NestedCvAll.AucMean.ToString("0.00")</b><span>nested 5×5-fold CV AUC (95% CI @_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooledCi95[1].ToString("0.00"))</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.Lockbox.Auc.ToString("0.00")</b><span>lockbox AUC, n = @_m.Lockbox.N (95% CI @_m.Lockbox.AucCi95[0].ToString("0.00")–@_m.Lockbox.AucCi95[1].ToString("0.00"))</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>@_m.LeaveOneHospitalOut.PooledAuc.ToString("0.00")</b><span>AUC on hospitals the model never saw</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="two">
        <RocChart Points="_m.Roc" Auc="_m.NestedCvAll.AucPooled" Label="(nested CV)" Operating="_m.OperatingPoints.Values" />
        <div>
            <h3>The trade-off, in people</h3>
            <p class="small">Out of 100 anaemic and 100 healthy children (nested CV):</p>
            <ul class="small">
                <li><strong>Cautious cut-off</strong> (pallor index ≥ @Pct(_model.Thresholds.Low)): catches
                    @Pct(_m.OperatingPoints["low"].Sensitivity) anaemic children but also flags
                    @Pct(1 - _m.OperatingPoints["low"].Specificity) healthy ones.</li>
                <li><strong>Strict cut-off</strong> (≥ @Pct(_model.Thresholds.High)): wrongly flags only
                    @Pct(1 - _m.OperatingPoints["high"].Specificity) healthy children but misses
                    @Pct(1 - _m.OperatingPoints["high"].Sensitivity) anaemic ones.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="small">No cut-off is good at both. That is why PaleCheck reports a pallor index and an overlap zone instead of "you are anaemic".</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Where the reference eyes fall</h3>
    <DistributionChart Healthy="_model.Reference.HealthyHistogram" Anaemic="_model.Reference.AnaemicHistogram"
                       HealthyLabel="Healthy (Hb ≥ 11)" AnaemicLabel="Anaemic (Hb < 11)" />

    <details>
        <summary>More checks: hospitals, subgroups, features, confounds, haemoglobin</summary>
        <div class="grid2">
            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table>
                    <caption>Leave-one-hospital-out</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th>Held-out hospital</th><th>n</th><th>AUC</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                    @foreach (var h in _m.LeaveOneHospitalOut.PerHospital)
                    {
                        <tr><td>@h.Hospital</td><td>@h.N</td><td>@(h.N < 10 ? "too few" : h.Auc?.ToString("0.00") ?? "–")</td></tr>
                    }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <caption>Subgroups (fairness)</caption>
                        <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>n</th><th>AUC</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                        @foreach (var s in _m.Subgroups)
                        {
                            <tr><td>@s.Group</td><td>@s.N</td><td>@s.Auc.ToString("0.00")</td></tr>
                        }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="table-wrap">
                    <table>
                        <caption>Feature-set choice (development only)</caption>
                        <tbody>
                        @foreach (var kv in _m.FeatureSetSelection)
                        {
                            <tr class="@(kv.Key == _m.ChosenFeatureSet ? "chosen" : "")"><td>@kv.Key</td><td>@kv.Value.ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
                        }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="small">
            <li><strong>Best single colour measure:</strong> @BestFeature() — the model uses the "@_m.ChosenFeatureSet" feature set (@_model.Features.Length features).</li>
            <li><strong>Confound check:</strong> predicting from image size alone gives AUC @_m.ConfoundCheck["image_size_only_auc"].ToString("0.00"),
                below the model; crop size carries a little signal and we report it rather than hide it.</li>
            <li><strong>Negative result:</strong> estimating the haemoglobin <em>number</em> does not work (error @_m.HbRegression.Mae.ToString("0.00") g/dL vs
                @_m.HbRegression.BaselineMae.ToString("0.00") for always guessing the average; r = @_m.HbRegression.PearsonR.ToString("0.00")). PaleCheck never shows an Hb estimate.</li>
            <li><strong>Severe cases</strong> (Hb &lt; 7) are not dramatically easier: AUC @_m.Subgroups.Last().Auc.ToString("0.00").</li>
        </ul>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="lighting">
    <h2>3 · The bulb matters more than the blood</h2>
    <p>
        Healthy and anaemic eyelids differ by just <strong>@_m.Lighting.DiseaseGap.ToString("0.0") units</strong> of redness (CIELAB a*) on average.
        We re-lit every reference eyelid with physically modelled lights:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>White card looks</th><th>Redness shift</th><th>× anaemia gap</th><th>Score swing, no card</th><th>With card</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
            @foreach (var il in _m.Lighting.Illuminants)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td>@il.Kelvin K @LightName(il.Kelvin)</td>
                    <td><span class="swatch" style="background: rgb(@il.CardWhiteRgb[0], @il.CardWhiteRgb[1], @il.CardWhiteRgb[2])"></span></td>
                    <td>+@il.ShiftUncorrected.ToString("0.0")</td>
                    <td><strong>@il.TimesDiseaseGap.ToString("0.0")×</strong></td>
                    <td>±@((il.ProbChangeUncorrected * 100).ToString("0"))</td>
                    <td>±@((il.ProbChangeWithCard * 100).ToString("0"))</td>
                </tr>
            }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        A warm bulb shifts redness <strong>@_m.Lighting.Illuminants[0].TimesDiseaseGap.ToString("0.0")×</strong> more than anaemia does.
        Because the white card sits in the same light, dividing by its colour (von Kries correction) cancels the cast.
        In simulation that is exact by construction; the <a href="lab">Lighting Lab</a> measures how well it works with a real phone camera.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
        Contrast features within one photo (e.g. the reddest vs palest tissue) moved only
        @_m.Lighting.Illuminants[0].RelativeShiftSd.ToString("0.0") SD under the warm bulb vs
        @_m.Lighting.Illuminants[0].AbsoluteShiftSd.ToString("0.0") SD for absolute colour.
        @if (_m.ChosenFeatureSet == "relative")
        {
            <span>On the development data they also predicted slightly better (AUC @_m.FeatureSetSelection["relative"].ToString("0.00") vs
                @_m.FeatureSetSelection["absolute"].ToString("0.00"), a difference well within noise), so the app's model now uses them and
                is less sensitive to lighting even before the card corrects it.</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span>On the development data they predicted anaemia less well (AUC @_m.FeatureSetSelection["relative"].ToString("0.00") vs
                @_m.FeatureSetSelection["absolute"].ToString("0.00")), so the card, not the features, has to solve lighting.</span>
        }
    </p>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="screening">
    <h2>4 · Should a photo decide who gets a blood test?</h2>
    <p>
        A screening app is only useful if it beats the simple alternative. In India the alternative already exists: under Anaemia
        Mukt Bharat, school students get a free yearly haemoglobin test. Move the sliders to see what happens to 1,000 people if a
        photo decides who gets tested.
    </p>
    <ScreeningCalculator Curve="_m.OperatingCurve" InitialCutoff="@((int)Math.Round(_model.Thresholds.Low * 100))" />
    <p>
        <strong>Our conclusion:</strong> where anaemia is this common, a photo should <em>never</em> replace the blood test, because
        every test it saves costs a missed case. PaleCheck is designed for the people the system misses: those not in school,
        those who skipped the test, adults. Its job is to get them to a test, not to decide whether they need one.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="meaning">
    <h2>5 · What this means</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Data first.</strong> Phone anaemia screening is not held back by AI models; it is held back by data: reused photos and uncontrolled light.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest framing.</strong> With AUC ≈ @_m.NestedCvAll.AucMean.ToString("0.00"), a single photo can nudge someone toward a blood test, but it cannot rule anaemia in or out. PaleCheck says exactly that.</li>
        <li><strong>The fix is cheap.</strong> A printed white card plus a fixed routine (same window, same time of day) turns a phone into a repeatable measuring tool. Tracking your <em>own</em> readings over time removes differences between people, the next biggest source of noise.</li>
        <li><strong>Next step.</strong> Collect a calibrated dataset of Indian teenagers with lab haemoglobin, using the protocol below, and re-validate.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <ul class="small">
        <li>Reference eyes are children aged @_m.Dataset.AgeMonths[0]–@_m.Dataset.AgeMonths[1] months from @_m.Dataset.Hospitals hospitals in Ghana; anaemia is defined as @_m.Dataset.AnaemiaDefinition. Teenagers in India have different thresholds (WHO: 12 g/dL for girls 12–14 and women; 13 g/dL for men 15+).</li>
        <li>The dataset has no colour reference, so the model learned from uncalibrated photos while the app applies card correction. Re-training on calibrated photos is required.</li>
        <li>The lighting experiment above is a simulation; real-camera validation is pending (Lighting Lab).</li>
        <li>Only @(_m.Dataset.Anaemic + _m.Dataset.Healthy + _m.Dataset.DemoHoldout) independent photographs survive the audit, so every AUC here has a wide confidence interval.</li>
        <li>The re-crop search compares each image with its nearest colour neighbours only, so a few copies may remain undetected.</li>
        <li>Not a medical device. Not clinically validated.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="methods">
    <h2>6 · Methods</h2>
    <ol class="small">
        <li><strong>Pixels:</strong> outlined tissue only; glare (all channels &gt; @_model.PixelRules.GlareMin) and shadow (all &lt; @_model.PixelRules.DarkMax) removed.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour:</strong> sRGB → linear → CIE XYZ → CIELAB (D65). @_model.Features.Length features: @string.Join(", ", _model.Features).</li>
        <li><strong>Model:</strong> L2-regularised logistic regression (λ = @_model.Lambda via inner 5-fold CV), written from scratch in numpy, so the whole model is @(_model.Features.Length + 1) numbers that run offline in the browser.</li>
        <li><strong>Validation:</strong> removal of identical, near-identical and re-cropped copies → 8-image demo hold-out → 70/30 development/lockbox split → nested CV on development for every choice → single lockbox evaluation → leave-one-hospital-out, subgroup and confound checks → bootstrap 95% CIs (2,000 resamples).</li>
        <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> blackbody illuminants from the Kang et al. (2002) Planckian-locus fit, von Kries relighting in linear sRGB.</li>
        <li><strong>Parity:</strong> the C# app re-implements every formula; unit tests check it reproduces Python's features to 10⁻⁹.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small">Reproduce: <code>python model/get_data.py</code>, <code>python model/train.py</code>, then <code>dotnet test</code>. Seed @("20261001").
        Continuous integration re-downloads the public dataset and re-derives every headline number on each change.</p>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="protocol">
    <h2>7 · Data protocol for a calibrated dataset</h2>
    <ol class="small">
        <li>Participants: volunteers with a haemoglobin result from a lab or digital haemoglobinometer taken within the last 7 days. Under-18s need written consent from a parent or guardian as well as their own agreement.</li>
        <li>Capture: same window, daylight, no flash, card beside the eye; 3 photos per eye.</li>
        <li>Store numbers only (colour features, card colour, lab Hb, age band, sex) — never photos or names. Export from <a href="history">History</a> as CSV.</li>
        <li>Analyse with the same pipeline; publish de-duplicated, calibrated results.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section class="panel">
    <h2>Sources &amp; credits</h2>
    <ul class="small">
        <li>Appiahene P. et al. (2023). CP-AnemiC: A conjunctival pallor dataset and benchmark for anemia detection in children. <em>Medicine in Novel Technology and Devices</em>. Data: <a href="@_m.Dataset.Source" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mendeley Data, DOI @_m.Dataset.Doi</a>, @_m.Dataset.License. We removed duplicate images; no other changes.</li>
        <li>NFHS-5 (2019–21): anaemia in 59.1% of girls aged 15–19 in India (via Scott et al., <em>Maternal &amp; Child Nutrition</em>, 2022).</li>
        <li>Anaemia Mukt Bharat operational guidelines, National Health Mission, Government of India.</li>
        <li>Kang B. et al. (2002). Design of advanced color temperature control system for HDTV applications. <em>J. Korean Physical Society</em>.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private Metrics? _m;
    private PallorModel? _model;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _m = await Data.Metrics;
        _model = await Data.Model;
    }

    private static string Pct(double v) => (v * 100).ToString("0");

    private string BestFeature()
    {
        var best = _m!.SingleFeatureAuc.MaxBy(kv => kv.Value);
        return $"{best.Key} (AUC {best.Value:0.00})";
    }

    private static string LightName(int k) => k switch
    {
        <= 2800 => "(yellow bulb)",
        <= 3600 => "(warm LED)",
        <= 4200 => "(neutral tube)",
        _ => "(cool white)",
    };
}
